# Image-specific per-package USE decisions.

# Breaks the sys-libs/libcap-ng <-> sys-process/audit build-time cycle.
# The cycle exists only because this profile sets 'bpf' globally: libcap-ng[bpf]
# build-depends on audit, and audit build-depends on libcap-ng. This is
# portage's own suggested cut, and it costs nothing here — libcap-ng's bpf
# support is for its capability-set parser, not for anything audit needs.
sys-libs/libcap-ng -bpf

# Breaks the media-libs/freetype <-> gnome-base/librsvg cycle.
# Portage offers '-svg' or '-utils'; '-utils' is the right cut. freetype's
# dependency is literally 'utils? ( svg? ( librsvg ) )' — librsvg is needed
# only to build the demo programs (ftview, ftbench, ftdump), not for the
# library's OT-SVG support. '-svg' would have cost colour-font rendering for
# nothing. Both flags are on globally: 'svg' from the graphics group, 'utils'
# from 'extras tools utils contrib extra'.
media-libs/freetype -utils

# Breaks the whole family of X -> pipewire -> ffmpeg -> X build cycles in one
# cut, rather than one ffmpeg flag per cycle. Every one of them routes through
# the same edge: openal -> pipewire -> ffmpeg -> openal, then
# libsdl2 -> pipewire -> ffmpeg -> libsdl2, and so on for anything ffmpeg
# build-depends on that also offers a pipewire backend.
#
# It is also the cheapest of the three cuts portage offers each time. From the
# pipewire ebuild itself:
#     # - ffmpeg is only used for pw-cat. We don't build the spa plugin ...
#     -Dffmpeg=disabled # Disabled by upstream and no major developments to
#                       # spa/plugins/ffmpeg/ since May 2020
# So the flag buys compressed-format support in the pw-cat CLI tool and
# nothing else; the SPA plugin it sounds like it enables is off upstream
# regardless. The alternatives each cost a real audio path: '-sdl' on ffmpeg
# drops ffplay, '-pipewire' on libsdl2 or openal drops their native backend.
media-video/pipewire -ffmpeg

# Overrides host.use, which carries 'x11-libs/libxcb doc' from this host's own
# package.use. That single entry re-enables doc against the profile's global
# '-doc' and builds a cycle out of nothing:
#   doxygen -> graphviz -> libX11 -> libxcb -> doxygen
# libxcb[doc] exists to generate its own API HTML with doxygen. A live image
# has no use for it, and dropping it also takes doxygen and graphviz out of
# the graph entirely.
# (This file sorts after host.use, so it wins; portage reads package.use/* in
# alphabetical order and the last matching entry applies.)
x11-libs/libxcb -doc

# Breaks tslib -> libsdl2 -> pipewire -> libv4l -> qtbase -> tslib.
# x11-libs/tslib is a resistive-touchscreen input library from the embedded
# world; a T14's touchscreen goes through libinput/evdev and never touches it.
# The flag reached qtbase through rare-flags.use, which is where the census
# moved it — this file is named 'zz-' so image decisions sort last and win
# over both host.use and rare-flags.use.
dev-qt/qtbase -tslib

# Breaks libjpeg-turbo -> virtual/jdk -> openjdk-bin -> harfbuzz -> glib
# -> docutils -> pillow -> libjpeg-turbo.
# The global 'java' flag reaching a low-level image codec: libjpeg-turbo[java]
# builds the TurboJPEG Java wrapper, which drags a whole JDK into the graph.
# Nothing in this image loads JPEGs through Java.
media-libs/libjpeg-turbo -java

# Breaks pillow -> harfbuzz -> glib -> docutils -> pillow, the one cycle in
# this image that portage can suggest no cut for: dev-libs/glib BDEPENDs
# dev-python/docutils unconditionally, and docutils BDEPENDs pillow the same
# way, so neither edge has a flag to turn off. Only pillow[truetype] does.
#
# Chosen over a two-pass build (merge pillow once without truetype, let the
# main run rebuild it with it) to keep the pipeline single-pass.
#
# What it costs: PIL/Pillow's ImageFont TrueType rendering, and with it
# app-text/calibre, which USE-depends on pillow[truetype] and is therefore
# dropped from PROF_LIVE_PKGS. matplotlib is unaffected — it carries its own
# freetype. Re-enable the flag and rebuild in the running system if needed.
dev-python/pillow -truetype

# Breaks zziplib -> libsdl2 -> libpulse -> gtk+:3 -> cups -> xdg-utils
# -> xmlto -> texlive-formatsextra -> texlive-core -> zziplib.
# zziplib[sdl] adds an SDL_rwops shim for reading zip archives through SDL.
# Nothing in this image reads zips that way, and the flag is only set because
# 'sdl' is global.
dev-libs/zziplib -sdl
